  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 AM EDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nPattern / Forecast Synopsis: \n\nStout rex blocking reinforced by a chonker of a 1037mb surface \nhigh over Quebec to remain overhead throughout the period... \nupper low over the Ohio Valley to undergo Miller B cyclogenesis \nand produce what looks like quite the impressive early season \nnor'easter... but the blocking 500mb ridge and surface will only\nbe forced into retrograde overhead through the weekend and into\nearly next week. As such, anticipating a prolonged period of \nsteady temperatures and drier weather across the region through \nthe weekend and into early next week. Meanwhile, longwave \ntroughing to settling along the Pacific coast over the weekend \ninto next week, which should lead to a classic El Nino \nsubtropical jet amplification and result in a series of Pacific \nmoisture rich waves ejecting into the Plains. Numerous question \nmarks regarding the northward extent of the associated deep \nmoisture plume from the aformentioned waves into next week, but \nnonetheless, there are signals for rain chances creeping into \nthe picture midweek next week and beyond.\n\nDetails: \n\nBig blue bubble of no trouble, the big H... whatever words to use \nfor high pressure, they will apply for at least the next 6 days \nworth of weather forecasting across the region. As such, a complete \nand utter lack of sensible weather anticipated... overnight forecast \ncalls for dark, gradually turning partly light again in the morning. \nAll George Carlin references aside, there are a couple things to \nwatch with this pattern will be RH levels and gradually drier ground \nconditions... could see a couple days approach problematic fire \nweather parameters as highs top out in the low to mid 70s for most \nthrough early next week. Overnight lows a product of quality diurnal \nresponse through the weekend... interior icebox locales in the 30s, \nmost elsewhere in the 40s... immediate coastal areas near 50. As \nsuch, could see our first run of frost headlines at times across \ninterior locales... NBM guidance is already dipping interior \ntemps 37 and lower in places Friday and Saturday nights... so \nwouldn't be surprising to see locally colder temps. From a climo\nstandpoint, late September is well within range for frost \nacross the region, so this isn't anything dramatically out of \nthe ordinary. \n\nThere are some rumblings of a change in pattern heading into and \npast midweek next week. Aforementioned Pacific coast trough will be \nquite efficient in drawing deep tropical moisture from the Gulf of \nCalifornia north and east into the Four Corners / southern Plains to \nstart next week. Certainly looks like a classic El Nino active \nsubtropical jet outburst. Some guidance members want to have any \nshortwave ejections become quite amplified as they move into the \nPlains and undergo surface cyclogenesis... and thus return moisture \nand rain chances to the region. Going to be completely honest here, \nif El Nino climatology is to be honored, the members of guidance \ndepicting minimal moisture intrusion and rainfall into the Great \nLakes may win out... one of the hallmarks of a strong El Nino is a \npersistent dry and warm ridge over the Great Lakes and Midwest... \nand given a lack of digging northern jet stream activity... I do \nhave a hard time seeing this deeper moisture making it much farther \nnorth than the southern Plains, mid Mississippi Valley, lower Ohio \nValley, and the Tennessee Valley. Time will only tell, but my gut \nthought is the forecast for rainfall trends drier with time if climo \nis any sort of indicator.",
